{{/*
Model pin for the crush-switchboard harness — MANAGED, not create_.

Same file, same rules, same reasons as dot_local/share/crush-signal — read that
header first; this one only records why a SECOND copy exists.

crush-switchboard points CRUSH_GLOBAL_DATA at its own data dir so its model pin
and session history do not collide with crush-signal's. That split is the whole
point of the separate harness, but it also means the crush-signal pin does not
cover it: the large/small selection lives ONLY in $CRUSH_GLOBAL_DATA/crush.json,
never in ~/.config/crush/crush.json (which carries providers, MCP and options
and no `models` key at all). Without this file crush does not fail and does not
prompt — internal/config/load.go defaultModelSelection() silently takes the
first enabled provider from the catalog order, across the five this box
configures, hyper included. The harness would report "Crush · GLM-5.2 (Z.ai)"
in `harness list` while running whatever that fallback resolved to, which is
the exact shape of the drift the crush-signal pin was converted to fix.

MANAGED, not create_, for that same reason: crush's TUI model picker rewrites
the target in place, and create_ ("write only if absent") means chezmoi never
has an opinion about it again. czu_reassert_targets in executable_czu-run.zsh
absorbs the rewrite so the managed render keeps landing.

Same pin as crush-signal — this agent's work (webhook triage, issue and PR
reads) is the same weight as its Signal work.

  large  glm-5.3-balanced  doorbell triage, via LiteLLM: one model name over a
                           Z.ai deployment and a Hyper one, failing over per
                           request so neither provider's quota strands the pool
  small  Qwen3.8-27B       titles and summaries on ai01's local GPU — free, and
                           no quota to exhaust

@joestump-agent 09/14/2026 - Was glm-5.3-flash on zai for both slots, with a
  note here saying "nothing here should ever point at hyper" because zai is
  quota-metered and "degrades to a hard stop instead of a bill". The hard stop
  came on 09/14: Z.ai's weekly cap emptied, all four workers in this pool died
  at once, and the forge queue sat ~20h undrained against a four-day reset. A
  hard stop that takes every unattended agent offline is not the cheap failure
  that note assumed. Both slots now go through LiteLLM — large to the balanced
  group, small to the free local Qwen.

@joestump 08/29/2026 - Added while reviewing #197. The harness landed with
  CRUSH_GLOBAL_DATA repointed but no pin at the far end, so every box except
  the one it was hand-seeded on would have run the fallback model.

@joestump-agent 08/30/2026 - large glm-5.2 -> glm-5.3-flash, per Joe. Both
  tiers are now the same model, which is the point: flash is a generation
  newer than 5.2 and cheaper against the same quota, and the note above
  claiming "the 5.3 tier buys nothing here" was written before 5.3-flash
  existed as an option for the large slot.

  `harness list` also described these as "GLM-5.2 (Z.ai)" while the harness
  itself carried no model pin at all -- the model has always come from THIS
  file via CRUSH_GLOBAL_DATA. The description strings are corrected in
  harness.toml.tmpl so the listing stops asserting a pin that does not live
  there.
*/ -}}
{
  "$schema": "https://charm.land/crush.json",
  "models": {
    "large": { "model": "glm-5.3-balanced", "provider": "litellm" },
    "small": { "model": "Qwen3.8-27B", "provider": "litellm" }
  }
}
